WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.923
Dimensionality: what the number of coordinates buys.

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:23.256
Embedding sizes in current use range from 384 (the popular open model all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ) through 1,024 (the default for Voyage AI’s current models) to 3,072 (OpenAI’s text-embedding-3-large and Google’s Gemini embedding models).

3
00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:29.851
More dimensions can hold finer distinctions, but they cost memory, storage, and search time linearly.

4
00:00:30.131 --> 00:00:36.616
Stored as 32-bit floats, one million 1,024-dimensional vectors take about 4.

5
00:00:36.896 --> 00:00:39.435
1 GB before any index overhead.

6
00:00:39.715 --> 00:00:47.864
Lesson 2 shows how Matryoshka-trained models let you keep only a prefix of the vector and trade a little quality for a lot of space.

7
00:00:48.144 --> 00:00:52.624
Your math background helps with a counterintuitive fact about high dimensions.

8
00:00:52.904 --> 00:01:05.299
Draw two random unit vectors in d dimensions: their cosine has mean 0 and variance 1/d, so in 1,024 dimensions nearly every random pair is close to orthogonal.

9
00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:16.309
Meaning in an embedding space shows up as structured, measurable departures from that baseline, which is why ranking works even when the absolute differences between scores look small.

10
00:01:16.589 --> 00:01:22.264
Real models are not random, though, and their scores rarely spread evenly over -1 to 1.

11
00:01:22.544 --> 00:01:33.872
SimCSE’s authors described pretrained embedding spaces as anisotropic (not spread evenly in all directions) and showed that contrastive training makes them more uniform.

12
00:01:34.152 --> 00:01:37.160
Different models end up with different score distributions.

13
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.435
The consequence is the most common embedding bug in practice, and it’s the subject of the pitfall below.
